{"action":"review","objective":"# IDEA-263 MacSearch spec: adversarial review, ROUND 5 of max 10\n\nYou are one of **two independent reviewers**, one Claude and one Codex. You will not see the other's output.\n\n**Loop state:**\n\n| Round | Revision | Codex | Claude |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | r2 | NO-GO, 1 MAJOR | GO, 9 MINOR |\n| 3 | r3 | NO-GO, 1 MAJOR | GO, 8 MINOR |\n| 4 | r4 | NO-GO, 2 MAJOR | GO, 6 MINOR |\n\nRound 4's two MAJORs were:\n1. The plain-rename fallback on volumes without `RENAME_EXCL` kept the overwrite race.\n2. An `EEXIST` retry that ends in identical content left a stale pending record.\n\nAll MAJORs since round 2 sit in the NF4 publication path. This round reviews **revision 5**. It is not announced as final.\n\n## Workspace\n- Greenfield, with no code repository. The only source is the pinned ProductDoc.\n- **READ-ONLY.** Write only scratch files under `/tmp/`.\n\n## The document under review\nProductDoc `SPECS/idea-263-spec`: doc `cmvb0lj1r000per173xpccuxf`, **revision 5** `cmvb3ea2a002zer17g4p8x98x`, 308 lines. The previous revision was `cmvb347z2002fer17er8fb5fm`. §1a is the normative authority for JP's decisions.\n\n## Goal and boundaries\n- **Goal (IDEA-263):** a native macOS app for JP's own use.\n  - Search a source folder recursively for media whose file name contains the keywords.\n  - Preview them in a slider with untick, then copy or move them on OK to `<dest>/<keyword>/<date>/`.\n  - Keep history in SQLite. The window is a Finder-like two-pane window.\n- **Binding decisions:** §1a.\n- **New JP decision (2026-10-16), binding:** on destination volumes without `RENAME_EXCL` (exFAT, SMB, …), MacSearch writes the destination file itself, created exclusively with `O_EXCL` and recorded as owned. After a crash, a half-written app-owned file under the final name is **accepted**; it is removed at the next start. Overwriting a file that is not the app's remains forbidden. Do not raise this accepted trade-off as a finding. Do check that the mechanism really never overwrites or deletes a foreign file.\n- **First usable result and acceptance:** increment 1 (§8) plus AC1–AC7.\n- **First practical proof:** AC5 and AC7. AC7 now also covers an exFAT `.dmg`.\n- **Scope delta r4→r5:**\n  - Removed: the `link` tier and the plain-rename fallback.\n  - Added: route C, explicit retry rules, and tests.\n  - The first usable result is unchanged.\n\n## What changed since round 4. Verify each fix against revision 5; do not trust this list.\n1. **[MAJOR] No overwrite fallback.** NF4 now has three routes. The `RENAME_EXCL` capability is determined once per run via `getattrlist` `VOL_CAP_INT_RENAME_EXCL` (F15).\n   - **Route A** (same-volume move, capability present): `renamex_np(src, dest, RENAME_EXCL)`.\n   - **Route B** (copy, or cross-volume move, capability present): exclusive temp file → pending record → `fcopyfile` + dates → `renamex_np(RENAME_EXCL)`.\n   - **Route C** (no capability, any copy or move): `O_EXCL` on the final name → pending record with `temp_path` equal to `dest_path` → copy + dates → `temp_path = NULL` → only then delete the source.\n   - There is no plain `rename` anywhere.\n2. **[MAJOR] Retry bookkeeping.**\n   - There is one attempt counter (3) per file, and a retry always updates the existing record.\n   - Route B does not repeat steps 2–4 on a retry; it reuses the temp file.\n   - A retry that ends in identical content deletes the own file and sets the existing record to `skipped_identical` (F19b and NF4).\n   - AC3a asserts exactly one record and no pending record left.\n3. **Metadata.** Both dates are set explicitly. F15 states preservation, and a failure is a warning. AC3a has a success case and a failure case.\n4. **Folder creation** happens after the `search_run` insert (F14), with an AC4 case.\n5. **Tests.** AC3a gains:\n   - two final-name collision cases, one with different and one with identical content;\n   - three `EEXIST`s;\n   - a route A collision;\n   - route C cases with injected capability: collision, a crash after a half copy, and a crash after `temp_path = NULL`.\n\n## Attack priorities\nFirst, necessity: can anything be removed or deferred without losing agreed acceptance or necessary protection? \"None\" is valid.\n\nThen correctness:\n1. Do the fixes hold? Look for new contradictions, for example:\n   - NF4 routes vs F15, F16, F17, F19b and F0d;\n   - crash windows between record updates and file operations in each route;\n   - whether crash cleanup can ever delete a file that is not the app's, or a complete file whose source is already gone.\n2. Is anything wrong or unimplementable on macOS? Examples: `getattrlist` `VOL_CAP_INT_RENAME_EXCL`, `renamex_np` on a same-volume move, `O_EXCL` on SMB.\n3. Missing coverage against §1a.\n4. Tests that could pass while their named defect is present.\n\n## Finding format: strict\n### [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <title>\n**Where:** · **Claim:** · **Reality:** · **Consequence:** · **Fix:** <repair / SCHRAP / defer, the smallest remedy>\n\n## Reply\nOpen with a short summary:\n- counts per severity;\n- the most important finding;\n- the scope assessment;\n- a per-item verdict on fixes 1–5 (held / did not hold / partially held).\n\nThen the findings. Give the verdict `GO` or `NO-GO` in `review.verdict`. GO means zero BLOCKER and zero MAJOR. A technical GO authorizes nothing further.","product_id":"cmvayn6jl01jesy7r4mucpvgn","publish":"artifact","reply_to":"mac:claude","requirements":{"access":"read","environment_keys":[],"runtime":"CLAUDE"},"response_format":"DispatchResult","review_documents":{"items":[{"doc_id":"cmvb0lj1r000per173xpccuxf","key":"doc","product_id":"cmvayn6jl01jesy7r4mucpvgn","revision_id":"cmvb3ea2a002zer17g4p8x98x","sha256":"7d5e35872c417a5656ef53794aff95675f3ac0716f6e3c46951d509f029fadaf","source":"product_doc","title":"IDEA-263 MacSearch specificatie r5"}],"version":1},"verification":"BLOCKER: wrong, insecure or unimplementable as written; building it produces a defect.\nMAJOR: a real gap causing rework or a wrong decision. MINOR: clarity or polish.\nSCHRAP is an action, not a severity. A demonstrated scope error can be MAJOR when it causes a wrong decision or substantial unnecessary work. A preference for fewer lines is not MAJOR. Necessary privilege boundaries remain necessary even for one operator; removing or deferring work must preserve agreed acceptance and protection.\nMINOR findings are fully compatible with GO. Do not manufacture a MAJOR to avoid appearing lenient; a false MAJOR costs a round. Weigh the narrowing trend honestly in both directions.\nGive exactly one verdict in review.verdict: GO or NO-GO, never COMMENT.","version":1}